<div class="about-research">
  <section class="about-story" aria-labelledby="about-story-leakage">
    <h3 id="about-story-leakage">Temporal leakage in LLM backtesting</h3>
    <p>
      Backtesting evaluates a model’s prediction ability by asking historical questions as if it were forecasting from an earlier time. Its validity
      breaks when applied to LLMs: post-cutoff knowledge from pretraining leaks into the model’s reasoning and inflates the backtest score. My work
      treats this problem as a whole: a <strong>theory</strong> of when the inflation can be measured and corrected, an
      <strong>interpretable audit</strong> with an inference-time defense, and a <strong>training method</strong> that teaches the model temporal
      discipline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="about-works-label">Selected works in this line</div>

  <article class="about-paper">
    <div class="about-paper-inner">
      <div class="about-paper-body">
        <div class="about-paper-figure">
          <img
            src="{{ '/assets/img/about_story/theme_leakage.png' | relative_url }}"
            alt="Temporal leakage causal mechanism and the double-benefit law"
          >
        </div>
        <div class="about-paper-meta">
          <div>
            <span class="about-paper-role">Measure &amp; correct the score</span>
            <span class="about-paper-nick">Leakage Audit</span>
          </div>
          <div class="about-paper-title">Temporal Leakage in LLM Backtesting: Measurement, Validation, and Adjusted Scores</div>
          <p class="about-paper-blurb">
            LLMs legitimately know more about times near their cutoff, so recency mimics leakage, and no passive backtest can separate recency and
            leakage from genuine skill. Leakage concentrates on hard questions, and even partial memorization captures most of the inflation (the
            “double benefit”). Two estimators—a known cutoff or a clean control model—yield a leakage-adjusted score, validated on twin models with
            planted leakage.
          </p>
          <div class="about-paper-venue">TMLR 2026</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

