  <div class="about-research-sub">
    <section class="about-story" aria-labelledby="about-story-fabrication">
      <h3 id="about-story-fabrication">Attack and defense of LLM forecasting systems</h3>
      <p>
        Retrieval-based LLM forecasters search the news for evidence, and the wide search that makes them strong also makes them depend on the
        authenticity of every article they retrieve. We study the weakest attacker this design allows—one who knows only the forecasting question and
        publishes a single fabricated article—and show that even frontier models and leading deployed forecasting systems follow it, while accuracy
        hides the harm.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="about-works-label">Selected works in this line</div>

    <article class="about-paper about-paper-wide">
      <div class="about-paper-inner">
        <div class="about-paper-body">
          <div class="about-paper-figure">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/about_story/theme_fabrication.png' | relative_url }}"
              alt="Directional attack: one fabricated article flips a stock forecast"
            >
          </div>
          <div class="about-paper-meta">
            <div>
              <span class="about-paper-role">Attack the evidence</span>
              <span class="about-paper-nick">One Fabrication</span>
            </div>
            <div class="about-paper-title">One Fabrication Is Enough</div>
            <p class="about-paper-blurb">
              An attacker who knows only the forecasting question publishes one fake news article. Once retrieved, it reverses 74–82% of opposing
              forecasts on every frontier model tested, and the architectures of two leading deployed forecasting systems retrieve it in about 90% of
              runs. A two-article version makes the model confidently wrong rather than uncertain, and the strongest published defenses are cheaply
              bypassed.
            </p>
            <div class="about-paper-venue">Preprint 2026</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
